//// 1.0.10-beta `00 · 04-js` D7 — `.length()` on an OPTIONAL string or array.
//// Measured by rakun's front 05 while writing a configuration reader.
////
//// commonJS turns `.length()` into JavaScript's `length` PROPERTY only for a
//// receiver inference resolved as a named `string` or `array`. A `?string` is
//// neither, so `xs.at(0)?.length()` and `es.at(0)?.key.length()` kept their
//// call parens and the emitted module called a number — `TypeError:
//// es.at(0)?.key.length is not a function`, at exit 1 — while erlang, which
//// needs no receiver type to lower a primitive method, printed it. A silent
//// backend divergence, so the cell asserts the VALUE.
////
//// It carried `.targets commonJS erlang` until `00 · 05-wasm` fixed the
//// carrier: `es.at(0)?.key.length()` answered **276** on wasm — the
//// optional-chained field of a record taken out of `.at()` read the wrong
//// word, because a record element was WRITTEN into a box and READ as a bare
//// pointer. The other two lines were right there all along (`5`, `2`). The
//// sidecar is gone and the cell runs on all four targets;
//// `run/optional_record_carrier.bp` is where that carrier is pinned in full.
////
//// NOT here, and deliberately: a field read off a `?Record` receiver. `val
//// first = es.at(0); if (first != null) { first.key.length(); }` is narrowing's
//// row, not this rename's — it was a call on commonJS until `!= null` learned
//// to rebind `first`, and `run/narrowing_null_check.bp` is where it is pinned
//// (`comptime/AGENTS.md` § The null test narrows). This cell stays the receiver
//// that was never tested at all.

type Entry(key: string) {}

pub fn main() {
    val words = ["alpha", "be"];
    @print(words.at(0)?.length());
    val es = [Entry(key: "abc")];
    @print(es.at(0)?.key.length());
    val maybe = words.at(1);
    if (maybe != null) { @print(maybe.length()); };
}
